(2,4),(4,2). That's 6 points. We need to cover them with 2 more lines (since we need total 5 lines). Each line can have at most 3 points. So we need to partition these 6 points into 2 triples each lying on a sunny line. Let's see if possible.\n\nWe need to find lines (sunny) that contain exactly 3 of these points each, and are disjoint from the previous three lines (i.e., not sharing any points). The uncovered points are:\n\n- (1,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,4)\n- (4,1)\n- (2,4)\n- (4,2)\n\nWe can try to find a line with slope 1: diff constant. Differences: (1,2): -1; (2,1): 1; (1,4): -3; (4,1): 3; (2,4): -2; (4,2): 2. So diff values: -1,1,-3,3,-2,2. No diff repeats thrice. So slope 1 cannot give triple.\n\nSlope -2: compute c = y + 2x: (1,2): 2+2=4; (2,1): 1+4=5; (1,4): 4+2=6; (4,1): 1+8=9; (2,4): 4+4=8; (4,2): 2+8=10. So c values: 4,5,6,9,8,10. No repeats thrice.\n\nSlope -1/2: compute d = x + 2y: (1,2): 1+4=5; (2,1): 2+2=4; (1,4): 1+8=9; (4,1): 4+2=6; (2,4): 2+8=10; (4,2): 4+4=12. No repeats thrice.\n\nThus these three slopes cannot cover the remaining points in triples. Could we use other slopes? Let's try slope 2 (p/q = 2/1). Equation y = 2x + c. Compute c = y - 2x: (1,2): 2-2=0; (2,1): 1-4=-3; (1,4): 4-2=2; (4,1): 1-8=-7; (2,4): 4-4=0; (4,2): 2-8=-6. So c values: 0 appears for (1,2) and (2,4) (two points). Others unique. So slope 2 line can have at most 2 points among these.\n\nSlope -3: y = -3x + c => c = y + 3x: (1,2): 2+3=5; (2,1): 1+6=7; (1,4): 4+3=7; (4,1): 1+12=13; (2,4): 4+6=10; (4,2): 2+12=14. c=7 appears for (2,1) and (1,4) (two points). So not triple.\n\nSlope 1/2: y = 0.5 x + c => 2y = x + 2c => x - 2y = constant. Compute e = x - 2y: (1,2): 1-4=-3; (2,1): 2-2=0; (1,4): 1-8=-7; (4,1): 4-2=2; (2,4): 2-8=-6; (4,2): 4-4=0. So e=0 appears for (2,1) and (4,2) (two points). Not triple.\n\nSlope 3: y = 3x + c => c = y - 3x: (1,2): 2-3=-1; (2,1): 1-6=-5; (1,4): 4-3=1; (4,1): 1-12=-11; (2,4): 4-6=-2; (4,2): 2-12=-10. No triple.\n\nSlope -1/3: y = -x/3 + c => 3y = -x + 3c => x + 3y = constant. Compute f = x + 3y: (1,2): 1+6=7; (2,1): 2+3=5; (1,4): 1+12=13; (4,1): 4+3=7; (2,4): 2+12=14; (4,2): 4+6=10. f=7 appears for (1,2) and (4,1) (two points). Not triple.\n\nThus seems impossible to partition remaining points into two triples with any slope? But we haven't exhausted all possible slopes. However, we can consider lines with p+q=1 or -1 or 2, but also other slopes with larger |p+q| may have at most floor((k+1)/2) = 3 points, but maybe they can have exactly 3 points. For k=5, bound is floor((5+1)/2) = 3. So any line with |p+q|=1 or 2 can have up to 3 points. But lines with |p+q|=3 could have at most floor((5-1)/3)+1 = floor(4/3)+1 = 1+1 = 2 points, so cannot have 3. So only lines with |p+q|=1 or 2 can have 3 points. So we need to consider all lines with |p+q|=1 or 2. The solution only considered three specific lines (p+q=2, p+q=-1, p+q=1). But there are many lines with p+q=1 or -1 or 2. For p+q=2, we have p = 2 - q. For q>0 integer, p integer. For q=1, p=1 (slope 1). For q=2, p=0 (invalid). For q=3, p=-1 (slope -1/3). For q=4, p=-2 (slope -1/2) but gcd(2,4)=2 not allowed. For q=5, p=-3 (slope -3/5). For q=6, p=-4 (gcd(4,6)=2 not allowed). So many possibilities with p and q coprime. Similarly for p+q=-2: p = -2 - q. For q=1, p=-3 (slope -3). For q=2, p=-4 (gcd 2,2=2 not allowed). For q=3, p=-5 (slope -5/3). For q=4, p=-6 (gcd 2,4=2 not allowed). So many possibilities.\n\nThus there are many lines with |p+q|=2 that could potentially have 3 points in T_5. The solution claims only three lines can have exactly floor((k+1)/2) points. That seems false. Let's test some other lines for k=5.\n\nWe need lines with p+q=2 (or -2) or p+q=1 (or -1) that intersect T_5 in exactly 3 points. Let's search manually for some lines.\n\nGeneral line: p x - q y = c, with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q. For a given p,q, the set of integer points on the line is (x0 + q t, y0 + p t). The sum x+y = (x0+y0) + (p+q) t. So the sum changes by p+q each step. For p+q = 2, the sum changes by 2 each step. So the line will intersect T_k at points with sums in a certain arithmetic progression with step 2. Since T_k includes sums from 2 to k+1 = 6, we can have at most floor((6-2)/2)+1 = floor(4/2)+1 = 2+1 = 3 points. So any line with p+q=2 can have up to 3 points. Similarly for p+q=-2, sum changes by -2 each step, same bound.\n\nThus any line with p+q=2 or -2 can have up to 3 points. The question is: can we find lines with p+q=2 (or -2) that have exactly 3 points in T_5, and are distinct from the three lines already used? Let's try to find such lines.\n\nWe need to find integer solutions (x,y) in T_5 satisfying p x - q y = c, with p+q=2. Let's param: p = 2 - q. So line equation: (2 - q) x - q y = c => 2x - q(x + y) = c => 2x - q s = c, where s = x+y. So for each point (x,y) in T_5, s is known. So the line condition is 2x - q s = c. For a given q, we can try to find c such that there are three points with distinct t values (i.e., three points with sums s1, s2, s3 spaced by 2). Since p+q=2, the sum changes by 2 each step. So we need three points with sums s, s+2, s+4 (or decreasing). Also the x coordinate changes by q each step (since x = x0 + q t). So we need to find a line with q>0, p=2-q, gcd(p,q)=1, and points in T_5.\n\nLet's try q=3, p=-1 (slope -1/3). Then line equation: -1*x - 3*y = c? Wait p x - q y = c => (-1)x - 3y = c => -(x + 3y) = c => x + 3y = -c. So line is x + 3y = constant. Let's compute possible constants for points in T_5. Compute x+3y for each point:\n\n(1,1): 1+3=4\n(1,2): 1+6=7\n(2,1): 2+3=5\n(1,3): 1+9=10\n(2,2): 2+6=8\n(3,1): 3+3=6\n(1,4): 1+12=13\n(2,3): 2+9=11\n(3,2): 3+6=9\n(4,1): 4+3=7\n(1,5): 1+15=16\n(2,4): 2+12=14\n(3,3): 3+9=12\n(4,2): 4+6=10\n(5,1): 5+3=8\n\nNow group by constant: 4: (1,1); 5: (2,1); 6: (3,1); 7: (1,2),(4,1); 8: (2,2),(5,1); 9: (3,2); 10: (1,3),(4,2); 11: (2,3); 12: (3,3); 13: (1,4); 14: (2,4); 16: (1,5). So each constant appears at most 2 points. So no triple.\n\nThus line x+3y = constant cannot have 3 points.\n\nNow q=5, p=-3 (slope -3/5). Equation: -3x -5y = c => 3x +5y = -c. Compute 3x+5y for points:\n\n(1,1): 3+5=8\n(1,2): 3+10=13\n(2,1): 6+5=11\n(1,3): 3+15=18\n(2,2): 6+10=16\n(3,1): 9+5=14\n(1,4): 3+20=23\n(2,3): 6+15=21\n(3,2): 9+10=19\n(4,1): 12+5=17\n(1,5): 3+25=28\n(2,4): 6+20=26\n(3,3): 9+15=24\n(4,2): 12+10=22\n(5,1): 15+5=20\n\nAll distinct, no triple.\n\nNow q=1, p=1 (slope 1). Already used.\n\nNow q=2, p=0 invalid.\n\nNow q=4, p=-2 (slope -1/2). Already used.\n\nNow q=6, p=-4 (gcd 2,6=2 not allowed). So only allowed q values for p+q=2 are q odd (since p=2-q must be coprime with q). For q odd, gcd(2-q,q) = gcd(2,q) = 1 if q odd (since q odd => gcd(2,q)=1). So all odd q are allowed. So q=1,3,5,7,... produce lines with p+q=2. For q=7, p=-5 (slope -5/7). Let's test q=7, p=-5. Equation: -5x -7y = c => 5x +7y = -c. Compute 5x+7y for points:\n\n(1,1): 5+7=12\n(1,2): 5+14=19\n(2,1): 10+7=17\n(1,3): 5+21=26\n(2,2): 10+14=24\n(3,1): 15+7=22\n(1,4): 5+28=33\n(2,3): 10+21=31\n(3,2): 15+14=29\n(4,1): 20+7=27\n(1,5): 5+35=40\n(2,4): 10+28=38\n(3,3): 15+21=36\n(4,2): 20+14=34\n(5,1): 25+7=32\n\nAll distinct.\n\nThus lines with p+q=2 beyond q=1,3,5,... seem not to have triples. But we haven't proven that none have triples. However, we can try to find a line with p+q=2 that passes through three points of T_5. The condition for three points is that there exist three points (x_i,y_i) with sums s_i = x_i + y_i forming arithmetic progression with step 2, and x_i = x_0 + q t_i, y_i = y_0 + p t_i. Since p+q=2, the sum changes by 2 each step. So we need three points with sums s, s+2, s+4. Let's try to find such a triple.\n\nWe can try to find a line with q=3 (p=-1) that passes through points with sums 2,4,6 maybe? Let's see if there are points with sum 2: (1,1). Sum 4: points (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). Sum 6: points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). For a line with p+q=2, the sum changes by 2 each step. So if we start at (1,1) sum 2, next sum 4, next sum 6. So we need points (x0,y0) with sum 2, then (x0+q,y0+p) with sum 4, then (x0+2q,y0+2p) with sum 6. For q=3, p=-1: Starting at (1,1) sum 2. Next point: (1+3,1-1) = (4,0) sum 4, but y=0 not positive integer (must be >0). So not allowed. Starting at (1,1) not good.\n\nTry start at (2,2) sum 4. Next point: (5,1) sum 6 (since (2+3,2-1) = (5,1)). That's valid. Next point: (8,0) sum 8 out of range. So we have only two points (2,2) and (5,1). Not three.\n\nTry start at (3,1) sum 4. Next: (6,0) invalid.\n\nTry start at (1,3) sum 4. Next: (4,2) sum 6 (valid). Next: (7,1) sum 8 out of range. So only two points.\n\nThus q=3 yields at most two points.\n\nNow q=5, p=-3: Starting at sum 2: (1,1) sum 2. Next: (6,-2) invalid. Starting at sum 3? Actually sum must be integer. Let's try start at sum 3? But T_5 includes sum from 2 to 6. So start at sum 3: points (1,2),(2,1). Let's try (1,2) sum 3. Next point: (1+5,2-3) = (6,-1) invalid. So no.\n\nTry start at sum 4: (1,3) sum 4. Next: (6,0) invalid. (2,2) sum 4 -> (7,-1) invalid. (3,1) sum 4 -> (8,-2) invalid.\n\nThus q=5 yields at most one point.\n\nThus seems only q=1 (p=1) yields three points (the diagonal). q=3 yields at most two points. So maybe indeed only three lines (p+q=2, p+q=-1, p+q=1) produce triples. But we need to check p+q=-2 lines as well. For p+q=-2, we have p = -2 - q. For q>0, p negative. For q=1, p=-3 (slope -3). Let's test line with p=-3,q=1: equation -3x - y = c => 3x + y = -c. Compute 3x + y for points:\n\n(1,1): 3+1=4\n(1,2): 3+2=5\n(2,1): 6+1=7\n(1,3): 3+3=6\n(2,2): 6+2=8\n(3,1): 9+1=10\n(1,4): 3+4=7\n(2,3): 6+3=9\n(3,2): 9+2=11\n(4,1): 12+1=13\n(1,5): 3+5=8\n(2,4): 6+4=10\n(3,3): 9+3=12\n(4,2): 12+2=14\n(5,1): 15+1=16\n\nGroup: 4: (1,1); 5: (1,2); 6: (1,3); 7: (2,1),(1,4); 8: (2,2),(1,5); 9: (2,3); 10: (3,1),(2,4); 11: (3,2); 12: (3,3); 13: (4,1); 14: (4,2); 16: (5,1). So max 2 points.\n\nNow q=3, p=-5 (since p+q=-2 => p = -2 - q = -5). Equation -5x -3y = c => 5x + 3y = -c. Compute 5x+3y:\n\n(1,1): 5+3=8\n(1,2): 5+6=11\n(