else. Let's list all possible slopes between pairs of points in the triangle. For each pair of points (a,b) and (c,d) with a<c (different columns), slope = (d-b)/(c-a). Since a,b,c,d are integers, slope is rational. We need to avoid slopes 0, infinite, -1. So we need to find a set of lines that avoid those slopes.\n\nWe can try to see if there is a covering with 4 lines where each line has at most one point per column. Let's try to assign points to lines such that each line has exactly one point in each column (i.e., each line has 4 points). Since we have 4 lines, that would cover 4*4 = 16 points, but we only have 10 points, so some lines will have missing points in some columns. But we can try to assign each line to have points in columns 1,2,3,4 (maybe some lines missing some columns). Let's try to find a set of 4 lines each with a point in column 1 (distinct points), and each line also has a point in column 2 (distinct points), etc. This is like a Latin square of order 4 with some missing entries.\n\nWe can try to assign a permutation for each line: For line i, assign a row index b_i(a) for each column a (if the point (a,b_i(a)) exists). The condition is that b_i(a) must be integer between 1 and n+1-a. Also for each column a, the values b_i(a) across i must be distinct (since each point is unique). So we need to fill a 4x4 array where column a has entries from the set {1,...,n+1-a} = {1,...,5-a}. For n=4, column 1 has values 1..4, column 2 has values 1..3, column 3 has values 1..2, column 4 has value 1. So we need to fill a 4x4 matrix with distinct entries per column, using the allowed values.\n\nOne possible assignment:\n\n- Column 1: assign values 1,2,3,4 to lines L1-L4.\n- Column 2: assign values 1,2,3 to three lines, and one line gets no entry (i.e., skip column 2).\n- Column 3: assign values 1,2 to two lines, other two lines skip.\n- Column 4: assign value 1 to one line, others skip.\n\nNow we need to ensure that for each line, the points (a,b_i(a)) are collinear. That is, the points assigned to a line must lie on a straight line.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines such that the set of points assigned to each line is collinear. This is a combinatorial geometry problem.\n\nWe can try to find a solution by solving a system of equations. Let’s denote line i has points (a,b_i(a)) for a in some subset of columns. For each line i, the points must satisfy a linear equation y = m_i x + c_i (since slope not infinite). Or vertical line x = constant (but vertical lines are non-sunny). So we need non-vertical lines (since sunny lines cannot be vertical). So each line i must have equation y = m_i x + c_i with m_i ≠ 0, m_i ≠ -1. Also m_i can be any rational number.\n\nGiven two points (a,b) and (c,d) on line i, we have m_i = (d-b)/(c-a). So for each line, the slope is determined by any pair of its points.\n\nThus we need to assign points to lines such that for each line, the slope between any two of its points is consistent and not 0, infinite, -1.\n\nGiven the small size, we can try to search manually for a covering. But we can also try to prove impossibility by parity or something.\n\nLet's attempt to find a covering for triangle size 4 with 4 sunny lines. We'll try to assign points to lines systematically.\n\nWe have 10 points. Let's label them:\n\nA1=(1,1), A2=(1,2), A3=(1,3), A4=(1,4)\nB1=(2,1), B2=(2,2), B3=(2,3)\nC1=(3,1), C2=(3,2)\nD1=(4,1)\n\nGoal: partition these points into 4 subsets (lines) such that each subset is collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nWe can try to find a partition where each line has at most one point per column.\n\nOne approach: Use lines that go through points in columns 1 and 2 (two points) and maybe also column 3 or 4.\n\nLet's list all possible sunny lines that contain at least two points from the set.\n\nWe can compute slopes between any two points and see if slope is not 0, infinite, -1.\n\nList all pairs (p,q) with p in column a, q in column c > a.\n\nCompute slope = (d-b)/(c-a). We'll list pairs and slopes.\n\nPairs:\n\n- A1 (1,1) with B1 (2,1): slope = (1-1)/(2-1) = 0 (non-sunny) -> discard.\n- A1 with B2 (2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1 (sunny). So line L(A1,B2) slope 1 passes through (1,1),(2,2). It also passes through (3,3) not in set, (4,4) not in set. So line L1 = {(1,1),(2,2)}.\n\n- A1 with B3 (2,3): slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2 (sunny). L2 = {(1,1),(2,3)}. It also passes through (3,5) not in set.\n\n- A1 with C1 (3,1): slope = (1-1)/(3-1) = 0 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A1 with C2 (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2 (sunny). L3 = {(1,1),(3,2)}.\n\n- A1 with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-1)/(4-1) = 0 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A2 (1,2) with B1 (2,1): slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A2 with B2 (2,2): slope = (2-2)/(2-1) = 0 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A2 with B3 (2,3): slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1 (sunny). L4 = {(1,2),(2,3)}.\n\n- A2 with C1 (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 (sunny). L5 = {(1,2),(3,1)}.\n\n- A2 with C2 (3,2): slope = (2-2)/(3-1) = 0 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A2 with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3 (sunny). L6 = {(1,2),(4,1)}.\n\n- A3 (1,3) with B1 (2,1): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2 (sunny). L7 = {(1,3),(2,1)}.\n\n- A3 with B2 (2,2): slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A3 with B3 (2,3): slope = (3-3)/(2-1) = 0 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A3 with C1 (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A3 with C2 (3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 (sunny). L8 = {(1,3),(3,2)}.\n\n- A3 with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3 (sunny). L9 = {(1,3),(4,1)}.\n\n- A4 (1,4) with B1 (2,1): slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3 (sunny). L10 = {(1,4),(2,1)}.\n\n- A4 with B2 (2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2 (sunny). L11 = {(1,4),(2,2)}.\n\n- A4 with B3 (2,3): slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A4 with C1 (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5 (sunny). L12 = {(1,4),(3,1)}.\n\n- A4 with C2 (3,2): slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\n- A4 with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1 (non-sunny). Discard.\n\nNow also consider pairs within column 2 and column 3 etc.\n\nPairs within column 2 (B1,B2): vertical line (non-sunny). B1,B3 vertical? No, same column, vertical line x=2 (non-sunny). B2,B3 vertical (non-sunny). So no sunny lines within same column.\n\nPairs within column 3 (C1,C2): vertical line x=3 (non-sunny). So no.\n\nPairs between column 2 and column 3:\n\n- B1 (2,1) with C1 (3,1): slope = (1-1)/(3-2) = 0 (non-sunny).\n- B1 with C2 (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1 (sunny). L13 = {(2,1),(3,2)}.\n- B2 (2,2) with C1 (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1 (non-sunny).\n- B2 with C2: slope = (2-2)/(3-2) = 0 (non-sunny).\n- B3 (2,3) with C1: slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2 (sunny). L14 = {(2,3),(3,1)}.\n- B3 with C2: slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1 (non-sunny).\n\nPairs between column 2 and column 4 (D1):\n\n- B1 (2,1) with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-1)/(4-2) = 0 (non-sunny).\n- B2 (2,2) with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 (sunny). L15 = {(2,2),(4,1)}.\n- B3 (2,3) with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1 (non-sunny).\n\nPairs between column 3 and column 4:\n\n- C1 (3,1) with D1 (4,1): slope = 0 (non-sunny).\n- C2 (3,2) with D1 (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1 (non-sunny).\n\nThus the list of sunny lines (with at least two points) is:\n\nL1: (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1\nL2: (1,1)-(2,3) slope 2\nL3: (1,1)-(3,2) slope 1/2\nL4: (1,2)-(2,3) slope 1\nL5: (1,2)-(3,1) slope -1/2\nL6: (1,2)-(4,1) slope -1/3\nL7: (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2\nL8: (1,3)-(3,2) slope -1/2\nL9: (1,3)-(4,1) slope -2/3\nL10: (1,4)-(2,1) slope -3\nL11: (1,4)-(2,2) slope -2\nL12: (1,4)-(3,1) slope -3/2\nL13: (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1\nL14: (2,3)-(3,1) slope -2\nL15: (2,2)-(4,1) slope -1/2\n\nAlso there are lines with three points? Let's check if any of these lines contain a third point from the set.\n\n- L1: (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1. Does it contain (3,3)? Not in set. So only two points.\n\n- L2: (1,1)-(2,3) slope 2. Does it contain (3,5)? Not in set.\n\n- L3: (1,1)-(3,2) slope 1/2. Does it contain (2,1.5)? Not integer.\n\n- L4: (1,2)-(2,3) slope 1. Does it contain (3,4)? Not in set.\n\n- L5: (1,2)-(3,1) slope -1/2. Does it contain (2,1.5)? Not integer.\n\n- L6: (1,2)-(4,1) slope -1/3. Does it contain (2,5/3)? Not integer.\n\n- L7: (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2. Does it contain (3,-1)? Not in set.\n\n- L8: (1,3)-(3,2) slope -1/2. Does it contain (2,2.5)? Not integer.\n\n- L9: (1,3)-(4,1) slope -2/3. Does it contain (2,7/3)? Not integer.\n\n- L10: (1,4)-(2,1) slope -3. Does it contain (3,-2)? Not in set.\n\n- L11: (1,4)-(2,2) slope -2. Does it contain (3,0)? Not in set.\n\n- L12: (1,4)-(3,1) slope -3/2. Does it contain (2,2.5)? Not integer.\n\n- L13: (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1. Does it contain (4,3)? Not in set.\n\n- L14: (2,3)-(3,1) slope -2. Does it contain (4,-1)? Not in set.\n\n- L15: (2,2)-(4,1) slope -1/2. Does it contain (3,1.5)? Not integer.\n\nThus each sunny line contains exactly two points from the set (except maybe some lines could contain three points if they align with a third point, but none do). So each sunny line can cover at most two points.\n\nThus to cover 10 points with 4 sunny lines, each covering at most 2 points, we can cover at most 8 points. The remaining points would need to be covered by non-sunny lines, but we have only 4 lines total, all sunny. So impossible. Wait, we have 4 lines total, all sunny. So each line can cover at most 2 points (since any line with two points is a sunny line, and we have enumerated all possible sunny lines with at most 2 points). So maximum coverage is 8 points, less than 10. Therefore covering all 10 points with 4 sunny lines is impossible. This proves k=4 impossible.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k≥4 impossible is correct.\n\nNow we need to verify the solution's answer set {0,1,3} is correct. We have shown constructions for k=0,1,3 for all n≥3. We have argued k=2 impossible (by the column n-2 forcing vertical line and then column n-1 and n forcing third sunny line). We have argued k≥4 impossible (by the triangle of size k cannot be covered by k sunny lines be